§2. THE MACACHOR HERMENEUTIC INVARIANT — FORMAL STATEMENT
================================================================================

THEOREM 2.1 (The Hermeneutic Invariant)
────────────────────────────────────────
Let E be any semantic extraction operator. Let D_n denote the nth derivative
of the scalar substrate. Then:

    (i)  INCOMPLETENESS: If E operates solely on D_n for n ≥ 2, then E is
         formally incomplete. ∃ ψ₁ ≠ ψ₂ such that E[D_n(ψ₁)] = E[D_n(ψ₂)].

    (ii) MINIMAL COMPLETENESS: If E operates on (ψ, D₁) where D₁ = ∇ψ, then
         E is minimally complete. ψ is uniquely determined up to the
         integration constant ψ(x₀), which is fixed by structural anchoring.

    (iii) STRUCTURAL EMPTINESS: If E operates solely on ψ (no derivative),
          then E captures the absolute but is dynamically empty — it contains
          no information about how meaning transforms across the domain.

COROLLARY 2.2 (The Emotion Degeneracy)
──────────────────────────────────────
Emotional states {sad, happy, mad, ...} are elements of the space of
third and fourth derivatives ∇³ψ, ∇⁴ψ. By Theorem 2.1(i), these states
are semantically degenerate without access to (ψ, ∇ψ). Two distinct scalar
fields can produce identical emotional signatures.

    Formally: ∃ ψ₁, ψ₂ with ‖ψ₁ - ψ₂‖ > ε but ‖∇³ψ₁ - ∇³ψ₂‖ < δ
    for arbitrarily small δ, given sufficiently different boundary conditions.

    Therefore: Emotional extraction without structural anchoring is
    mathematically impossible.

