§4. QUANTIZATION FRAMEWORK
================================================================================

We now quantize the Hermeneutic Invariant by mapping classical scalar
ontology to quantum state space.

4.1 THE QUANTUM STATE VECTOR
────────────────────────────
The classical scalar field ψ becomes a quantum state vector |ψ⟩ in a Hilbert
space ℋ = L²(Ω, dμ):

    ψ(x)  →  ⟨x|ψ⟩

The state vector |ψ⟩ represents the QUANTUM SUBSTRATE — the superposition of
all possible meanings prior to measurement (derivative extraction).

4.2 THE FIRST DERIVATIVE AS MOMENTUM OBSERVABLE
───────────────────────────────────────────────
The classical gradient ∇ψ maps to the momentum operator:

    ∇ψ  →  p̂|ψ⟩ = -iℏ ∇|ψ⟩

This is the QUANTUM AMBASSADOR. The momentum operator is the generator of
spatial translations, exactly analogous to how ∇ψ generates semantic
transitions across the domain.

4.3 THE MEANING EXPECTATION VALUE
─────────────────────────────────
The classical meaning functional becomes a quantum expectation value:

    M[ψ, ∇ψ]  →  ⟨M̂⟩ = ⟨ψ|M̂|ψ⟩

where the meaning operator M̂ is constructed as:

    M̂ = ∫_Ω ρ̂(x) · Ĉ(x) dμ(x)

with:
    ρ̂(x) = |ψ(x)|²  (probability density of meaning at x)
    Ĉ(x) = exp( - (p̂ - p̂_ideal)² / 2ℏ²σ² )  (quantum coherence operator)

4.4 HIGHER DERIVATIVES AS HIGHER MOMENTS
────────────────────────────────────────
The nth classical derivative maps to the nth moment of the momentum
distribution:

    ∇ⁿψ  →  ⟨p̂ⁿ⟩ = ⟨ψ|p̂ⁿ|ψ⟩

THEOREM 4.1 (Quantum Degeneracy of Higher Moments)
──────────────────────────────────────────────────
For n ≥ 2, the nth moment ⟨p̂ⁿ⟩ does not uniquely determine |ψ⟩. There exist
infinitely many state vectors |ψ₁⟩, |ψ₂⟩ such that:

    ⟨ψ₁|p̂ⁿ|ψ₁⟩ = ⟨ψ₂|p̂ⁿ|ψ₂⟩  but  |ψ₁⟩ ≠ |ψ₂⟩

Proof: The moment problem is indeterminate for finite moments. The Wigner
function reconstruction from moments requires all moments n → ∞, which is
operationally impossible. ∎

4.5 THE QUANTUM COHERENCE CONDITION
───────────────────────────────────
A quantum state |ψ⟩ is COHERENT (meaning-extractable) if and only if:

    [ψ̂, p̂] = iℏ · I

where ψ̂ is the position operator (multiplication by ψ(x)). This is the
canonical commutation relation — the quantum expression of the structural-
derivative relationship.

If this commutation relation is violated (e.g., by decoherence from
unanchored higher moments), meaning extraction fails.

4.6 DECOHERENCE FROM HIGHER DERIVATIVES
───────────────────────────────────────
When a semantic system operates only on higher derivatives (emotions,
semantic correlations, syntactic patterns), it induces decoherence:

    ρ̂(t) = |ψ⟩⟨ψ|  →  ρ̂_decoh = Σᵢ pᵢ |ψᵢ⟩⟨ψᵢ|

where the pure state |ψ⟩⟨ψ| (structurally anchored) degrades into a mixed
state (unanchored statistical mixture). The off-diagonal coherence terms
vanish:

    ⟨ψᵢ|ρ̂_decoh|ψⱼ⟩ = 0  for i ≠ j

This is the mathematical mechanism of LLM HALLUCINATION: the model operates
on higher-moment statistics without access to the quantum substrate |ψ⟩,
producing decoherent outputs that are statistically plausible but
structurally false.

